From 5c709a118fbd95424fb4414b24e3b26d42e36c0d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.local>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 09:31:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(security): file-by-file audit - NaN validation, CSV
 injection, write resilience

Full audit of every backend file, prioritized and fixed in order:

1. storage.py - write-path resilience (most critical finding)
   store_raw_batch() and store_validation_report() had NO error handling at
   all around their Supabase calls, unlike catalog()/list_studies()/
   count_observations() which already degrade gracefully. These two run on
   EVERY collection call for EVERY agent - a single transient network blip
   (the same WinError 10035 already seen in production) would crash the
   whole POST /api/v1/collection/{sector} request.
   Correction to an earlier overstatement: table_clean and table_public
   writes in _store_supabase_pipeline_batch already HAD try/except with a
   clear RuntimeError on failure - that part of the initial finding was
   wrong. What was genuinely missing there was retry-before-giving-up: a
   single blip failed immediately with no second chance.
   Fix: added a shared _with_retry() helper (3 attempts, short backoff).
   Best-effort writes (raw archive, audit log) retry then log-and-continue.
   The critical writes (table_clean, table_public) retry then still raise
   RuntimeError if truly persistent - real data loss must stay visible,
   not be silently swallowed.
   main.py: all 8 collection endpoints now also catch RuntimeError -> 503
   (they only caught ValueError before), so a persistent failure returns a
   clear "temporarily unavailable" response instead of an unhandled 500.

2. schemas/observation.py - reject non-finite values
   `value: float` accepted NaN/Infinity with no constraint. agents/base.py's
   own outlier-detection code already anticipated NaN (math.isnan checks),
   confirming this is a real scenario, not theoretical - yet such a value
   would still reach the DB and the public API. json.dumps() emits the
   literal tokens NaN/Infinity for these, which is invalid per the JSON
   spec and would silently break strict parsers used by researchers
   exporting this data. Added a field_validator rejecting non-finite values
   (a ValidationError, which agents/base.py already catches as ValueError,
   so a single bad record is skipped rather than crashing the batch).
   Also added a source_url validator: not rendered as a clickable link
   anywhere today, but a bare, unvalidated string field is exactly the kind
   of latent gap that becomes an XSS/javascript: URI vector the day someone
   adds a "view source" link without re-checking this field.

3. storage.py list_observations() - sanitize legacy bad data
   Since the validator above only prevents *new* NaN/Infinity from being
   written, added a sanitization pass in list_observations() (the common
   path feeding both /api/v1/observations and both export endpoints) that
   nulls out any non-finite value already in the DB from before this fix,
   rather than trusting allow_nan=False and letting the export endpoints
   500 on legacy rows.

4. services/export.py - CSV/formula injection (CWE-1236)
   csv.DictWriter wrote raw field values with no escaping. A cell value
   starting with =, +, - or @ is interpreted as a formula by Excel/Sheets/
   LibreOffice when the exported CSV is opened - relevant here specifically
   because these exports are aimed at researchers/students who will open
   them in a spreadsheet app. Values from external sources (indicator/
   source/notes) aren't fully trusted. Fixed by prefixing any such cell
   with a leading single-quote (the standard mitigation), applied per-cell
   via csv.DictWriter's row-by-row path instead of writerows().

5. main.py - Content-Disposition filename injection
   The `sector` query param (unvalidated) was interpolated directly into
   the Content-Disposition header for CSV/JSON exports. Added
   _safe_filename_part() stripping anything but alphanumeric/hyphen/
   underscore before it reaches the header value.

6. agents/base.py - silent archival failures
   _archive_raw()'s `except Exception: pass` gave zero trace if local
   archival ever failed (disk full, permissions, ...). Now logs a warning
   instead - still non-fatal, but no longer invisible.

7. cli.py - path traversal via --sector
   The export command built a filesystem path directly from --sector with
   no validation (e.g. --sector "../../.env" could write outside
   data/exports/). Low severity (requires local shell access to the CLI
   already), fixed by sanitizing to alphanumeric/hyphen/underscore only,
   same helper pattern as the main.py fix.

Flagged but NOT fixed here (need your input / infra access, not a code
patch):
- Render deployment has no committed Procfile/render.yaml, so I can't
  verify --forwarded-allow-ips trusts Render's proxy. If it doesn't,
  request.client.host (used as the rate-limit key everywhere) may resolve
  to Render's proxy IP for every visitor, meaning all visitors could share
  one rate-limit bucket. Needs checking directly in the Render dashboard.
- No SQL file in the repo actually creates table_public/table_raw/
  table_clean/table_logs/table_rapports - schema.sql defines an unrelated,
  seemingly-unused `observations` table instead. The real schema exists
  only in the live Supabase project. Whether table_public.secteur has a
  CHECK constraint blocking 'health'/'energy' needs to be checked directly
  in Supabase (query provided in the previous message).
- The synchronous Supabase client (create_client) called from async FastAPI
  routes (via run_in_threadpool) is the likely root cause of the recurring
  WinError 10035, beyond just the missing try/except this patch fixes.
  A real fix would mean migrating to create_async_client and making
  storage.py's methods properly async - a significant rewrite, not
  something to do inside a single patch without confirming first.

Tests (tests/test_data_integrity_fixes.py, 10 new, all passing):
- NaN/Infinity rejected, finite values still accepted.
- javascript: URI source_url neutralized to None, https:// URL kept as-is.
- CSV export escapes =/+/-/@-prefixed cells, leaves normal text untouched.
- _with_retry: succeeds first try, retries-then-succeeds, and exhausts all
  attempts before raising (verified call counts, not just outcomes).

Full suite: 29/29 passing. Also verified end-to-end via TestClient: a demo
health collection stores correctly, the resulting CSV export has a clean
Content-Disposition header, and GET /observations returns the data.
